<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Tio</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1880 CE (approx.) · capital Mbe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Teke kingdom on the plateaus north of Malebo Pool, whose king signed the 1880 treaty that founded French rule on the Congo.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Tio or <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/teke">Teke</a> kingdom formed on the sandy plateaus north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malebo-pool">Malebo Pool</a> by the 15th century. Portuguese sources of the 16th century call it Anziku and describe wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a> for control of the Pool, the hub of river trade between the interior and the Atlantic coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king, known as the Makoko, ruled from Mbe as a sacred figure whose power came from the national shrine of Nkwe Mbali and the royal nkobi, a box of sacred relics. Real power was shared with territorial lords who controlled trade at the Pool. The kingdom traded slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> downriver in exchange for cloth, guns and shells, and was famed for its raffia textiles.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1880 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makoko-iloo-i">Makoko Iloo I</a> signed a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-savorgnan-de-brazza">Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza</a> placing his lands under French protection, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> its foothold on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo</a> and its new post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a>. The kingship survives as a ceremonial office in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-tio.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Tio" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Central_Africa_1350CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1880 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mbe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Iloo I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Teke religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Teke</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, <a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Anziku, Teke Kingdom, Makoko</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1640&amp;focus=kingdom-of-tio" aria-label="Kingdom of Tio on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5077 2024 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Tio lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5397 2280L5262 2418"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5262 2418h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5397 2280h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5397 2280h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mbe. <a href="/atlas?year=1640&amp;focus=kingdom-of-tio">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

